{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/.codex/skills/codex-claude-council/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: codex-claude-council\ndescription: Run a multi-round research and challenge council between Codex and Claude Code on a specified objective, seeking evidence-backed agreement while preserving genuine disagreements. Use when the user requests a joint council, debate, or iterative co-review with Claude Code.\n---\n\n# Codex and Claude Code council\n\nClarify the objective, scope, constraints, evidence and decision needed from the request and workspace. Apply the repository's instructions and existing authorization; a council does not authorize unrelated changes or override a user's decisions. State what each reviewer must leave out of scope.\n\n## Independent investigation\n\n- Codex investigates the primary sources, code and relevant prior decisions itself. Give Claude Code the same objective, scope and primary materials, but avoid supplying a preferred conclusion in its initial assignment where independence matters. Ask it to cite exact files and concrete evidence, distinguish facts from hypotheses, and propose the cheapest decisive checks and counterexamples.\n- Run Claude Code with the model the user chose or, if none, an available appropriate model. For read-only review restrict tools to `Read,Glob,Grep` and a read-only permission mode. Preserve the exact handoff, session ID, streaming output and terminal result in the task's work area. Never bypass permissions.\n- A typical local invocation is `claude -p --model <model> --permission-mode plan --tools Read,Glob,Grep --output-format stream-json --verbose < handoff.txt > round-1.jsonl 2> round-1.stderr`. Validate installed CLI options first. Continue the same session using `--resume <session-id>` with the next challenge. Check final result and exit status; tool activity or a partial stream is not completion. Poll the running process and surface meaningful progress; do not kill a working reviewer merely because it is taking minutes. Report actual failures or access limitations accurately.\n\n## Challenge and converge\n\nCompare independent findings against the underlying material yourself before presenting them as established. Send Claude the specific disagreements, counterexamples and your own analysis, and ask it to challenge its prior conclusions as well as yours. Repeat until each material claim and proposed next step is explicitly agreed with its supporting evidence, or the remaining uncertainty or disagreement is identified. Agreement is not proof: identify what would falsify the shared hypothesis and which claims remain untested. Do not force consensus or invent a reviewer response.\n\nKeep rounds proportionate and purposeful. A reviewer who is still investigating should be allowed to finish. If meaningful progress stops because of an actual failure or impasse, preserve the trace and tell the user what remains unresolved.\n\n## Deliver\n\nGive the user the conclusion in plain language, concrete source examples, agreed causes versus hypotheses, unresolved disagreements, priority and the next discriminating test. Preserve the exchange and a concise decision record where the task's existing documentation belongs. Update dependent human-readable views when the workspace requires it. Implement changes only within the user's authorized scope and after the applicable project diagnosis and review requirements; do not change frozen baselines or treat the council's agreement as approval for a new product decision.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/DESIGN.md; ls -la /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/DESIGN.md; ls -la /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/'","aggregated_output":"# Citation-only judge version: closed id lists in the answer format (design, D712)\n\n**Alex's decision (D712, 2026-11-21):** \"开引用格式新版本 (推荐)\". The question offered: every id field of the judge's answer takes\na closed list built from the request; a $0 check on stored data that accepted answers still pass and the bad ids are excluded; any\npaid test asks him again.\n\n**Scope:** the answer format's four id fields only. Not changed:\n- the prompt text;\n- the request text;\n- the rung field;\n- `check_answer` and the retry;\n- the link direction (a separate design, D711).\n\n## 1. Census (stored data, $0)\n\n- **Refusals.** Every refused judge answer on the stored runs is an id-membership error: an id outside the list its field allows.\n  - 17 refusals in 5,408 distinct attempts: old-1, old-2 and old-3, plus the fresh calls of both pilot arms (Codex r37b; Claude's\n    script `id_typo_census.py`).\n  - All 5,389 committed answers fit the lists.\n  - The classes:\n    - a one-character copy of a listed evidence id: e3620 → e362, e3110 → e3111;\n    - a garbled evidence id (`e393`);\n    - a theme id in `cites_views` or in an evidence field;\n    - a dependency id used as an evidence id.\n- **List sizes per stored judge request** (`enum_size_census.py`; counting evidence twice, for cites and for push ids, plus themes and\n  dependencies), over 5,415 distinct requests (old-1, old-2, old-3; the pilots' fresh rows only, since their archives copy old-2's):\n  median 50–58 on the old runs and 160–183 on the pilots' fresh reader requests; p99 up to 573; max 909 (old-1 call 5652: 451\n  evidence ids, 7 themes, no dependency). 51 requests exceed 500. The two existing push enums add 6 values.\n- **Provider limits** (OpenAI structured outputs guide, read 2026-11-21):\n  - \"up to 1000 enum values across all enum properties\";\n  - above 250 values, \"the total string length of all enum values cannot exceed 15,000 characters\";\n  - all names, enum values and consts together at most 120,000 characters;\n  - `minItems`/`maxItems` supported;\n  - `$defs`/`$ref` supported;\n  - the guide does not say whether an empty enum is allowed, or whether a referenced enum counts once.\n\n## 2. Mechanism\n\nThe closing line of every request already forbids the wrong ids (`judging.py` lines 180–182: \"cites may name only the evidence ids\nlisted above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; … a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or\ncites_views\"). The answer format accepts any string in those fields (`schema_for`, lines 55–75). So the prose rule is present and the\nmodel still breaks it: about 0.3% of answers overall, and 2.6% of linked readers' answers, whose requests carry a dependency block\nbeside the themes.\n\nThe code already decides membership after the answer (`check_answer`). Moving the same closed set into the format makes a wrong id\nimpossible to write: code owns identity (AGENTS §2). The judge still decides what matters.\n\n## 3. The change\n\n- **Versions** (the D694 pattern: a new name for a changed judge, the old ones byte-identical):\n  - `judging-v9`: judging-v7's text and request, with closed lists.\n  - `judging-v10`: judging-v8's text and dependency block, with closed lists.\n  - The prompt files are copies, pinned to v7's sha. `SUPPORTED_VERSIONS` and the frozen tests extend; `schema_for('judging-v7')` and\n    `schema_for('judging-v8')` keep `JUDGING_SCHEMA_SHA`.\n  - Config: `poc-v121` = poc-v118 with `rejudging.prompt_version` = judging-v9 (the product with the fix). No config names v10 until\n    the link-direction design; v10 is built so the $0 test covers the dependency-block shape.\n  - Alternative (the council's call): a `rejudging.closed_lists` field instead of two names. Claude's lean is names: the archive,\n    harnesses and frozen tests already key on the version.\n- **The lists come from the request text that is sent,** read by the same parsers `check_answer` uses, so the format and the check\n  agree by construction:\n  - evidence ids: `evidence_shown_in(user)`;\n  - theme ids: `themes_shown_in(user)`;\n  - dependency ids: the resolved dependencies, exactly the set `check_answer` allows. A test asserts that this set equals the ids the\n    text lists.\n- **The fields:**\n\n  | Field | Closed list | With an empty list |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `cites` items | evidence ids | `maxItems: 0` |\n  | `pushes[].evidence_id` | evidence ids | `pushes` gets `maxItems: 0` |\n  | `cites_views` items | dependency ids | `maxItems: 0` |\n  | `theme_pushes[].view_id` | theme ids | `theme_pushes` gets `maxItems: 0` |\n\n  Ids are listed sorted.\n- **Representation:** to settle before the build.\n  - (a) A plain enum per field: the stored max is 909 + 6 = 915 values, under 1,000.\n  - (b) One `$defs` evidence enum referenced by both evidence fields: 464 if it counts once, which is unverified.\n  - (c) A `pattern` alternation, as the pre-v74i extraction admission schema did (`folds_into`, removed with the frozen prompt). It\n    falls outside the enum count but under the 120,000-character total.\n  - Claude's lean: (a). It is the plainest, and every stored request fits.\n- **Over the limit:** a request whose lists exceed the provider's limits is refused before the send (`JudgementFormatError` → the\n  day's `judging_failed`, resumable). There is no silent fallback to open strings. No stored request reaches the limit.\n- **Building it:** `schema_for(prompt_version, evidence_ids=None, dependency_ids=None, theme_ids=None)`.\n  - In `round_of_judgements`, each view's schema is built beside its prompt (`prompts[v]` gains the schema), and `ask` sends it, the\n    retry included.\n  - For v7/v8 the lists are ignored and the schema is the frozen one.\n  - The schema name stays `judgement` (harnesses read it).\n\n## 4. Tables\n\n### 4.1 State × event\n\n| State / event | What happens | What is written; which date |\n|---|---|---|\n| First judgement (no previous conclusion) | the lists are built as for any request | the same as now |\n| No dependency (all of poc-v121) | `cites_views` has `maxItems: 0` | — |\n| No theme | `theme_pushes` has `maxItems: 0` | — |\n| No evidence shown (all hidden) | `cites`/`pushes` have `maxItems: 0`; such a view stays eligible only if evidence is in force | — |\n| A refusal on another check (rung, changed, stance) | the retry carries the same schema | as now: the refused row is retired |\n| Double refusal | the day fails, resumable; on resume the same text gives the same schema | as now |\n| Resume of a failed day | the same text and schema; already judged views are not re-judged | as now |\n| A theme closes or a dependency resolves between prompt and answer | the lists are frozen in the sent request; `check_answer` reads the same text | — |\n| Concurrent judge workers | one schema per view, built from that view's text | — |\n| The provider refuses the schema (HTTP 400) | a transport error → `judging_failed` → resumable | no answer row |\n| Lists over the limit | refused before the send, as above | no call |\n| The config changes between runs | the version is in each call's config record; the schema is in the archive key | a new key per request |\n| A valid id that names the wrong source | not detectable by code | measured separately (§6) |\n\n### 4.2 Value × reader\n\n| Value | Reader | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| The per-request schema | `model.build_request`, then `request_identity` (archive key) | every judge request under v9/v10 gets a new key: no stored answer is reused; a paired run needs its own replay proof (Codex r37b) |\n| The schema name `judgement` | `paid_run.is_judgement_request`, `july_test` | unchanged |\n| The schema | `preflight.py` (`SCHEMAS['judge']` = v7's) | unchanged for stored v7 rows; v9 rows are checked by `check_answer` as now |\n| The answer | `vocabulary.judgement_answer`, `_record` | the same shape |\n| The input size | cost | about 450 characters at the median, up to about 7,300 at the max, priced at $0.10 per million input tokens: negligible; measured in the $0 test |\n\n### 4.3 New path × old checks\n\n| Old check | Under v9/v10 |\n|---|---|\n| `check_answer`: evidence membership, deps, themes | still runs; never fires on a membership error the format holds; kept as the owner of the rule |\n| Rung range, first judgement must say changed, rung moved needs changed, stance fields non-empty | unchanged (out of scope) |\n| Frozen tests (prompt sha, schema sha) | v7/v8 unchanged; v9/v10 pinned: prompt sha = v7's; a schema test on a fixed list |\n| D359: refuse unbuilt versions | `SUPPORTED_VERSIONS` extended to the two names only |\n| Launchers: which config runs where | poc-v121 a product config (like poc-v118); v10 is named by no config |\n\n### 4.4 Prior decisions\n\n- D354 (the frozen judge; a change is a new version Alex opens) and D712 (opened).\n- D359 (the code builds only named versions).\n- D694 (v8 = v7's text, a new name for a request change).\n- D198 (the closed set is what the request showed).\n- D711 (the census and the recommendation).\n- The pre-v74i extraction admission schema constrained `folds_into` to the shown ids by pattern. It was removed with the frozen prompt\n  (commit 248ef1b4), not rejected by any decision found.\n\n## 5. The $0 test (registered before it runs)\n\n- **T1, the product path** (old-2 replayed under poc-v121).\n  - The harness transport answers each judge request with old-2's stored answer for the same system and user bytes.\n  - Every judge request's text must be byte-equal to old-2's; only the schema may differ.\n  - The transport validates the stored answer against the request's schema with a standard-library validator for the subset used\n    (object, array, string enum, integer, boolean, required, `additionalProperties: false`, `maxItems`).\n  - Pass:\n    - the replay completes and equals old-2's store, as the `neither` pass does;\n    - every accepted old-2 answer validates;\n    - each of old-2's 4 refused answers fails validation on the id that `check_answer` refused.\n- **T2, every stored shape** (offline).\n  - For every stored judge request of old-1, old-2, old-3, control-1 and linked-1, the lists are rebuilt from its text (v10 for\n    linked-1's dependency block) and the stored answer validated.\n  - Pass: all 5,389 committed answers validate; all 17 refused answers fail on their bad id.\n- **T3, empty lists.** A view with no dependency (or no theme) gets `maxItems: 0`. An answer naming one id fails; an answer with\n  `[]` passes.\n- **T4, the limits.**\n  - The max list size over all stored requests is printed with its enum count and characters, under the provider's limits.\n  - A synthetic request over the limit raises before any send.\n- **Mutants**, each caught by a test that passes on the unmutated code:\n  - the push field left open;\n  - lists built from the ledger instead of the text;\n  - `maxItems` dropped for an empty list;\n  - the validator's enum check removed.\n- **Prompt size:** the schema's characters and tokens before and after, median and max.\n- **The suite, the frozen tests and `docs/check.py`.**\n\n## 6. What the $0 test cannot show\n\n- Whether, with the lists closed, the judge picks the intended source. The e3111 case could become e3110 or e3112.\n- Whether the format changes the forecasts.\n\nA paid measurement shows both, and it is Alex's to open (D712). One way: re-send the stored refused requests and a matched set of\naccepted ones under v9, and read the cited sources beside the forecasts.\ntotal 28792\ndrwx------@ 251 lchingen  wheel      8032 Nov 21 16:54 .\ndrwx------@   5 lchingen  wheel       160 Nov 16 16:16 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x@   2 lchingen  wheel        64 Nov 18 08:00 .held\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel    199017 Nov 19 16:59 DECISIONS.head.md\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel      3679 Nov 19 16:59 TRIES.head.md\ndrwxr-xr-x@   3 lchingen  wheel        96 Nov 20 08:00 a3-mutants\ndrwxr-xr-x@   3 lchingen  wheel        96 Nov 16 06:30 a3-packet-dry\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel  11893701 Nov 20 14:39 api-library.json\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel      4859 Nov 19 16:15 articles.json\ndrwxr-xr-x@   2 lchingen  wheel        64 Nov 21 08:00 astra-gate1-key\ndrwxr-xr-x@   8 lchingen  wheel       256 Nov 16 20:23 astra-mutants\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel     14720 Nov 17 17:42 b35-files.txt\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel     52236 Nov 17 17:41 backup-pop.txt\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel       133 Nov 17 23:23 blinding-3-key.json\n-rw-------@   1 lchingen  wheel     55578 Nov 17 17:40 branch-ids.txt\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel     24614 Nov 17 17:40 branch-inspan.txt\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel      1333 Nov 17 17:40 branch-only-inspan.txt\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel     32558 Nov 17 17:40 branchonly-hits.txt\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel      4064 Nov 20 17:08 build_pack.py\ndrwxr-xr-x@   2 lchingen  wheel        64 Nov 21 08:00 busyprobe\ndrwxr-xr-x@   5 lchingen  wheel       160 Nov 17 22:39 cards\ndrwxr-xr-x@   3 lchingen  wheel        96 Nov 15 20:22 carried-202203\ndrwxr-xr-x@   4 lchingen  wheel       128 Nov 17 18:19 census\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel      1714 Nov 17 23:14 census_reasons.py\n-rw-r--r--@   1 lchingen  wheel      3029 Nov 17 23:14 census_requests.py\ndrwxr-xr-x@   7 lchingen  wheel       224 Nov 18 13:11 chain-dry\ndrwxr-xr-x@   7 lchingen  wheel       224 Nov 18 13:35 chain-dry-10\ndrwxr-xr-x@   7 lchingen  wheel       224 Nov 18 13:39 chain-dry-11\ndrwxr-xr-x@   7 lchingen  wheel       224 Nov 18 13:43 chain-dry-12\ndrwxr-xr-x@   7 lchingen  wheel       224 Nov 18 13:44 chain-dry-13\ndrwxr-xr-x@   7 lchingen  wheel       224 N